  "local_coverage": {
    "title": "Data coverage and the 'Unknown' level",
    "category": "samui",
    "short": "The island level is only given when every critical factor has current data.",
    "body": "Six factors are critical: El Nino, water/rain, heat, heavy rain/flood, cyclone/wind and sea state. `coverage` lists how many factors have data, which are missing, which are stale, and which critical ones are missing.\n\n**Limits.** Coverage says whether data arrived, not whether the data is right. Model forecasts can still be wrong even when fresh.",
    "howToRead": "If one critical factor has no data or only old data, the overall level shows 'Unknown' instead of a number, and the headline names what is missing. 'Unknown' is not 'Normal': it means the app cannot tell. The phrase 'No immediate threat on Samui' only appears when all critical data is present and fresh.",
    "whyItMatters": "A wrong 'Normal' could make you skip preparing or miss the moment to leave. When a source is down, it is safer to say so plainly and point you to the TMD and ferry operators directly.",
    "related": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "tmd.go.th",
        "url": "https://www.tmd.go.th/en/warning-and-events/warning-storm"
      },
      {
        "title": "disaster.go.th",
        "url": "https://www.disaster.go.th/"
      }
    ]
  },
  "local_headline": {
    "title": "The Samui headline",
    "category": "samui",
    "short": "One paragraph: the level, the El Nino state, the local numbers with dates, what is on watch, and the next seasonal risk.",
    "body": "Built from the factors, in this order: level and El Nino state ('Prepare -- strong El Nino (ONI +1.80 °C JJA 2026; RONI +1.36 °C; Nino 3.4 +3.0 °C week of 16 Sep)'); the local picture ('No immediate threat on Samui: rain 103% of normal (90 d to 18 Sep), waves max 0.5 m (forecast to Sat 26 Sep), no cyclone within 800 km (nearest 2,002 km)'); factors at Watch or Prepare; missing data; and the next risk of the season ('NE-monsoon heavy rain and rough seas Oct-Dec 2026; water stress Feb-Apr 2027'). `headline_local` is the same text without the first sentence (reused by the briefing).\n\n**Limits.** The headline summarises; each factor card holds the full explanation and sources.",
    "howToRead": "'No immediate threat' is only written when every critical factor has current data and none is at Act or Leave. Otherwise the headline names the hazards or the missing data and where to check by hand.",
    "whyItMatters": "The El Nino 'Prepare' is about the 2027 dry season; the local numbers say whether anything threatens the island this week.",
    "related": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "tmd.go.th",
        "url": "https://www.tmd.go.th/en/warning-and-events/warning-storm"
      },
      {
        "title": "cpc.ncep.noaa.gov",
        "url": "https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml"
      }
    ]
  },
  "local_level_floor": {
    "title": "'At least' level (level floor)",
    "category": "samui",
    "short": "The lowest level the island can be at, computed from the factors that do have data.",
    "body": "`level_floor` applies the same rules as the full level (maximum of the factors, compound hazards, 'leave' rules) to the factors that are known.\n\n**Limits.** The floor can hide a higher real level if the missing factor is the dangerous one (for example, no wave data during a storm).",
    "howToRead": "Read 'At least Prepare (El Nino)' as: even without the missing data, you should already be at 'Prepare'. The real level may be higher, never lower. Actions and triggers shown while the level is Unknown are based on this floor.",
    "whyItMatters": "During a strong El Nino some signals (the event itself, a cyclone report) arrive even when a local weather feed is down: the floor keeps them useful.",
    "related": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "tmd.go.th",
        "url": "https://www.tmd.go.th/en/warning-and-events/warning-storm"
      }
    ]
  },
  "local_season_logic": {
    "title": "Season-aware risk rules",
    "category": "samui",
    "short": "Some thresholds weigh differently depending on Samui's season.",
    "body": "Samui's rain year: northeast monsoon October-January (November wettest, about half of the year's rain October-December); dry season February-May (February driest); southwest monsoon June-September, when the island is sheltered.\n\n**Limits.** Normals come from ERA5 (about 30 km cells), which underestimates rain at Samui's stations; comparisons are ERA5 against ERA5, so the bias largely cancels. The 7-day antecedent rain uses model analyses, not gauges.",
    "howToRead": "Water: in the dry season the 180-day rain window (which holds the last monsoon refill) is used if it is worse than the 90-day one; in October-December a rain deficit during a strong El Nino is at least 'Prepare', because the reservoirs refill only then; a rain-only 'Act' needs at least 100 mm really missing, not just a low percentage of a tiny dry-season normal. Flood: heavy rain forecast on ground soaked by 150 mm or more over the past 7 days raises the flood level by one; 250 mm in a week alone is 'Watch'. Sea: TMD wave warnings for the Andaman Sea only are ignored (they do not concern the Gulf).",
    "whyItMatters": "El Nino makes the February-April 2027 dry season the water danger window, while floods still come with the northeast monsoon (for example the November-December 2024 floods in the South and the 2011 and 2017 floods).",
    "related": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "open-meteo.com",
        "url": "https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api"
      },
      {
        "title": "open-meteo.com",
        "url": "https://open-meteo.com/en/docs"
      },
      {
        "title": "tmd.go.th",
        "url": "https://www.tmd.go.th/en/warning-and-events/warning-storm"
      }
    ]
  },
  "retention": {
    "title": "Data retention (what is kept, what is deleted)",
    "category": "data",
    "short": "Official bulletins and measurements are kept; news and posts are deleted after 180 days, closed map events after 30 days.",
    "body": "A daily housekeeping job. It deletes news, social and research items older than 180 days (official bulletins are kept forever), map events no source has listed for 30 days, and keeps the last 200 run records per source. Hourly buoy temperatures (the TAO/TRITON series) older than one year are folded into one daily mean per day; every daily and monthly series (indices, ERA5, gauges) is kept as it is. Once a week it compacts the database file (SQLite VACUUM).\n\n**Limits.** Deleted news cannot be recovered from the tracker (the original article usually still exists at its link). Daily and monthly measurement series are never pruned; for buoy data older than a year only the hour-by-hour detail is gone (the daily mean and the count of hours it came from stay).",
    "howToRead": "The status entry `maintenance` shows when it last ran, how many rows it removed and when the file was last compacted.",
    "whyItMatters": "It keeps the tracker fast and the disk small on a laptop, without losing the history that matters: all index and measurement series and every official bulletin stay.",
    "related": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "SQLite VACUUM",
        "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/lang_vacuum.html"
      }
    ]
  },
